Chronic Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) Reactivation
Integrative & Functional medicine
Unrelenting severe fatigue, Recurrent sore throat/cervical lymphadenopathy, Exacerbation following stress, Elevated EBV Early Antigen IgG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-004
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unrelenting fatigue, low-grade fever, and significant cervical pain requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute bacterial lymphadenitis or peritonsillar abscess.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient with worsening sore throat, painful swollen neck glands, night sweats, and profound exhaustion. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude airway compromise, severe acute bacterial infection, and acute hepatosplenomegaly, preventing clinical deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My throat is killing me, my neck glands are huge, and I am so exhausted I physically cannot lift my head off the pillow."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 5-day history of a recurrent, mild sore throat that acutely worsened 12 hours ago, accompanied by unrelenting, profound generalized fatigue and severe exhaustion entirely unalleviated by sleep. The patient notes tender, persistently palpable anterior and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy that has become intensely painful today. Symptoms include low-grade fevers and distinct subjective night sweats over the past two nights. The patient also reports generalized, migratory muscle aches, vague joint pain, and marked short-term memory deficits (brain fog). The patient states this severe systemic crash followed an intense period of psychological stress at work last week.
Functional Status: Bedbound for the past 24 hours due to profound fatigue and myalgias.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to prepare meals due to severe exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears mildly toxic, lethargic, and is speaking softly to avoid throat pain. No stridor or respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: HEENT exam reveals mild, non-purulent pharyngeal erythema without tonsillar exudate or uvular deviation. Neck exam demonstrates highly tender, enlarged anterior and posterior cervical lymphadenopathy bilaterally. Abdominal exam reveals mild, distinct left upper quadrant fullness with tenderness on deep palpation, indicating mild splenomegaly, and a smooth, palpable liver edge (mild hepatomegaly). Musculoskeletal exam shows generalized tenderness without joint effusion. The clinical triad of unrelenting severe fatigue, recurrent sore throat, and cervical lymphadenopathy is present, strongly suggesting viral reactivation. Diagnostic confirmation awaits evaluation of Epstein-Barr virus Early Antigen (EA) IgG.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is cluttered with water glasses; patient has easy access to a phone.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Currently taking Thyroxine for known Hashimoto's thyroiditis; no dosing errors noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) Reactivation - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Streptococcal Pharyngitis / Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of gross purulent tonsillar exudate, uvular deviation, "hot potato" voice, or severe unilateral throat pain.

Acute HIV Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of diffuse macular rash or generalized mucocutaneous ulcerations, though definitive ruling requires serology.

Acute Cytomegalovirus (CMV) Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinical similarity, but less likely to present with this degree of pronounced cervical lymphadenopathy compared to EBV; requires serology to definitively differentiate.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe worsening of throat pain, fever, and extreme fatigue. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need to evaluate for potential airway compromise (peritonsillar abscess) or acute bacterial lymphadenitis. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to visualize the oropharynx, palpate for acute splenic enlargement, and rule out a severe, life-threatening bacterial infection. The identification of mild splenomegaly and severe cervical lymphadenopathy confirmed that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of splenic rupture from unrecognized organomegaly or progressive airway occlusion. This encounter unequivocally satisfies the criteria for urgent after-hours assessment (MBS Items 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommend strict bed rest and avoidance of all contact sports or heavy lifting to prevent splenic rupture. Prescribed NSAIDs (Ibuprofen 400mg TDS PRN) for lymph node analgesia and antipyresis. Recommend comprehensive viral serology (EBV EA IgG, EBNA IgG) via regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the profound energy deficit, initiate strict energy conservation techniques. Action: Divide essential daily tasks (e.g., showering, eating) into 10-minute maximum blocks followed by 30 minutes of strict supine rest. Consequence: Preventing physical overexertion strictly limits the physiological stress response, rewarding the patient with a gradual lifting of the unrelenting fatigue. Action: Drink 250ml of water every time a meal is consumed. Consequence: Consistent hydration thins mucosal secretions, directly soothing the recurrent sore throat.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days for blood test requisitions and ongoing monitoring of organomegaly.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if experiencing sudden, severe left-sided abdominal pain, shoulder tip pain, difficulty breathing, or inability to swallow liquids.

